abstract="The widespread adoption of Virtual Reality (VR) has highlighted the need for realistic 3D avatars that accurately represent users. However, Head-Mounted Displays (HMDs) often occlude critical facial regions, such as the eyes and eyebrows, making it challenging to create expressive and lifelike avatars. While direct face-to-face interaction in VR is still limited by HMD occlusion, researchers and technologies are emerging that enable increasingly realistic facial expression capture and avatar representation. Generative adversarial networks (GANs) have been widely used in image restoration tasks due to their excellent image generation capabilities, providing new possibilities for realistically restoring complete faces under HMD occlusion. In this work, we proposed a occluded face reconstruction method based on conditional GAN. The method guides the generator to infer and complete the occluded upper region based on the visible lower half of the facial region and facial landmarks; at the same time, the discriminator prompts the generator to generate more realistic and identity-consistent facial images by evaluating the difference between the generated image and the real image. The proposed work demonstrate the model on a custom dataset of HMD-occluded faces. The proposed solution enables the creation of immersive 3D avatars for VR applications.",
isbn="978-3-032-08600-6"
